This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. Furthermore, we have demonstrated the existence of a family of signed ground state solutions for the related nonlocal equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} by applying a variant of the concentration compactness principle. Moreover, we have proven the uniqueness of the normalized ground state solution for both equations. Lastly, we have provided the asymptotic behavior of the signed ground state solutions as $\mu\to 0$. Additionally, we have established the existence and uniqueness of the normalized ground state solution for a related equation involving $Q$ when $\lambda$ is sufficiently small. Conversely, we have shown the existence of a non-trivial solution for a different equation involving $Q$ when $\lambda$ is sufficiently large. Furthermore, we have provided several key estimates to accomplish these findings. To our knowledge, this study is the first to investigate the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation under the
